    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The lateral ventricle subventricular zone (SVZ) is a common area of spread for both pediatric and adult gliomas, but the mechanism mediating this spread has been poorly understood. Diffuse Intrinsic Pontine Glioma (DIPG), a childhood glioma, begins in the pons and spreads to the SVZ, making DIPG an ideal model for studying the invasion mechanism of gliomas. Preliminary research elucidated that four proteins—pleiotrophin, secreted protein acidic and rich in cysteine (SPARC), SPARC-like protein 1, and heat shock protein 90B—are secreted from SVZ neural precursor cells and promote glioma invasion. One of these proteins, pleiotrophin, is a neurite outgrowth-promoting factor and has been previously implicated in adult glioblastoma cell migration. By understanding the role of pleiotrophin along with the other three proteins, we could elucidate the mechanism of glioma invasion. To further explore the role of these factors, we knocked down the four proteins using both in vitro and in vivo methods. Subsequently, we explored the effect of knocking down a known pleiotrophin receptor, protein tyrosine phosphatase receptor type ζ, on DIPG invasion. The results of these experiments show that pleiotrophin, in complex with its binding partners, has a chemoattractant effect leading to DIPG invasion of the SVZ. These findings demonstrate the role of these four secreted factors in glioma interactions and may lead to future therapeutic targets to limit glioma invasion.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
